{
  "configuration_id": "config_001",
  "reason": "With no prior evidence, the most important uncertainty is whether multimodal fusion (RNA + image) provides any benefit over single-modality approaches, and what a reasonable baseline accuracy looks like. Config_001 (multimodal, singleprofile, pca_n2000, resnet50, concat) is the simplest and most interpretable multimodal configuration: it uses a fast linear RNA representation (PCA), a standard pretrained CNN (ResNet50), and the most basic fusion (concatenation). This establishes a clear baseline that isolates the core question—does combining modalities help at all?—without confounding from complex encoders, multiprofile aggregation, or learned fusion weights. If it performs well, subsequent experiments can systematically upgrade individual components (e.g., scGPT for RNA, ViT for image, weighted fusion). If it underperforms, the result tells us the bottleneck is in representation quality or fusion strategy, guiding more targeted exploration. As experiment 1 of 10, this maximizes information per trial by anchoring the search space with a well-understood reference point."
}

